    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll start by clustering the data by vendor and employee as you suggested. \n\nOne thing that's bothering me is that these discrepancies are very small—sometimes just a few euros—and they seem to pop up at random intervals. It's almost like they're just \"hiding\" in the noise of the larger transactions. Do you have any advice on how to pinpoint these specifically when they're so tiny and scattered? I want to make sure I'm not just chasing ghosts.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the discrepancies are tiny and scattered, you're essentially hunting for a needle in a haystack, which is a task for sophisticated data filtering. I recommend using statistical outlier detection methods, such as calculating the standard deviation for the 'miscellaneous' category and flagging any transaction that falls outside two or three standard deviations from the mean. This will automatically filter out the normal noise, leaving you with the statistically anomalous, even if they are only a few euros. You can also try time-series analysis to see if the randomness has any underlying cyclical pattern, even if it's subtle.",
      "source": "target",
